{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>You shall have no other Elohim before Me</b> Rashi wrote: <b>לא יהיה לך</b> — why is this stated? Since it is stated <b>לא תעשה לך</b> ('you shall not make for yourself'), I know only that one may not make them; from where do I know that one may not keep one already made? Scripture states: <b>לא יהיה לך</b>. And this is indeed a baraita cited in the Mekhilta (here). If so, this would be only a negative commandment, a warning against maintaining idolatry in one's possession, and it does not carry the death penalty of the court. And why would He have placed the prohibition of maintaining — which is only a negative commandment — before the prohibitions of bowing down and serving, which carry the penalty of excision and the death penalty of the court?\n\nAnd in my opinion the halakhah does not follow this baraita; it is cited as the opinion of an individual. For thus we have learned in the Sifra (beginning of Kedoshim): <b>ואלהי מסכה לא תעשו לכם</b> (Leviticus 19:4) — could one infer that they may make other ones? Scripture states: 'לא לכם.' I know only 'for yourselves'; could they make them for others? Scripture states: 'לא תעשו' — neither for yourselves nor for others. From here they said: one who makes an idol for himself transgresses two prohibitions — לא תעשו and לא לכם. Rabbi Yosei says: three — לא תעשו, לא לכם, and לא יהיה. You see that Rabbi Yosei is an individual against the majority, who says that לא יהיה לך is a warning against making images, and according to the first Tanna it is not so.\n\nAnd the correct interpretation, even according to the plain sense, based on the language of 'והיה ה׳ לי לאלהים' (Genesis 28:21), 'להיות לכם לאלהים' (Leviticus 11:45), is as follows: that we shall have none but HaShem; other Elohim — from among all the angels of the heights and all the host of heaven that are called Elohim — shall not be ours, in the sense of 'זובח לאלהים יחרם בלתי לה׳ לבדו' (Exodus 22:19). This is a prohibition against believing in any one of them, against accepting it upon oneself as a deity, and against saying to it 'you are my El.' And such is the view of Onkelos, who rendered: 'אלה אחרן בר מני' (another deity besides Me). And know that everywhere Scripture says 'אלהים אחרים' the intent is: others besides the glorious HaShem. And this expression applies to the acceptance of divinity or to worship — that is, it says: you shall not accept upon yourselves any deity but YHWH alone. But when Scripture speaks of making, it does not say 'אחרים,' perish the thought, but rather 'ואלהי מסכה לא תעשו לכם' (Leviticus 19:4), 'אלהי מסכה לא תעשה לך' (Exodus 34:17). And it calls them by the name Elohim because they are made with the intention that they be one's deity, but concerning them Scripture says: 'כי לא אלהים המה כי אם מעשה ידי אדם עץ ואבן' (Isaiah 37:19).\n\nBehold, in the second commandment He first warned us not to accept for ourselves any lord from among all the Elohim besides YHWH, and afterward He said that we shall not make a graven image or any likeness to bow down to them or serve them in any form of worship whatsoever. And therefore He did not say 'ולא תשתחוה להם' (and you shall not bow down to them) as a separate clause, for it is attached to the making: He restrained us from bowing down to them. Behold, they are all prohibitions against idolatry, and all of them are punishable by death. And there is in this verse no prohibition against one who makes a hundred images without worshipping them; but below He warns against this: 'אלהי כסף ואלהי זהב לא תעשו לכם' (Exodus 20:23), and likewise 'אלהי מסכה לא תעשה לך' (Exodus 34:17), 'לא תעשו לכם אלילים' (Leviticus 26:1)." "And the sense of <b>על פני</b> ('before My face') is like 'אם לא על פניך יברכך' (Job 1:11), 'ועתה הואילו פנו בי ועל פניכם אם אכזב' (Job 6:28). He warns: do not make for yourself other Elohim, for they are על פני — before My face, for I look and gaze at every time and in every place upon those who do so. A thing done in a person's presence while he stands watching is called 'על פניו,' as in 'ותעבר המנחה על פניו' (Genesis 32:22), and likewise 'וימת נדב ואביהוא ויכהן אלעזר ואיתמר על פני אהרן אביהם' (Numbers 3:4) — for Aaron their father was seeing and standing there, and in Chronicles (1 Chronicles 24:2): 'וימת נדב ואביהוא לפני אביהם ובנים לא היו להם.' Thus He says: do not make for yourself other Elohim, for I am constantly present with you and see you in hidden things and in the open.\n\nAnd by way of truth, you will understand the secret of the 'faces' (פנים) from what we have written (Ramban on Exodus 3:2), for at this station Scripture warns: 'פנים בפנים דבר ה׳ עמכם' (Deuteronomy 5:4). And know the secret of the word 'אחרים,' and the entire verse will come according to its plain sense and its meaning. And Onkelos likewise alluded to this, as it is written: 'לא תעשון אתי' (Exodus 20:23), and so forth.\n\n<b>כי אנכי ה׳ אלהיך</b> — I alone, and it is not fitting that you associate others with Me. And I am El, mighty, who has the power of a deity, and zealous, for I will be zealous in giving My honor to another and My praise to graven images. And nowhere in Scripture does the language of jealousy (קנאה) appear with the glorious Name except regarding idolatry alone. And the Rav said in the Guide for the Perplexed (1:36) that you will not find in all the Torah and all the books of the Prophets the language of wrath (חרון אף), nor the language of anger (כעס), nor the language of jealousy (קנאה) except regarding idolatry alone. But this is not so: in Kedoshim it says 'ויחר אף ה׳ במשה' (Exodus 4:14); 'ויחר אף ה׳ בם וילך' (Numbers 12:9); and it is written (Job 42:7): 'חרה אפי בך ובשני רעיך כי לא דברתם אלי נכונה כעבדי איוב.' But regarding the language of jealousy, it is indeed true. And thus they said in the Mekhilta (here): in jealousy I exact punishment for idolatry, but in other matters I am gracious and merciful.\n\nAnd in my opinion He mentions jealousy in connection with idolatry only regarding Israel, and the reason for the jealousy is that Israel is the treasured possession of the glorious HaShem, whom He set apart for Himself, as I explained above (Exodus 19:5). Behold, if His own people, His servants, turn to other Elohim, HaShem is jealous of them, just as a man is jealous of his wife when she goes to others, and of his servant when he makes for himself another master. And Scripture does not say thus concerning the other nations, to whom He allotted the host of heaven.\n\nAnd here I will mention what the Scriptures teach regarding idolatry, for there were three kinds. The first: they began to worship the angels, which are the separate intelligences, because they knew that some of them have dominion over the nations, as it is written (Daniel 10:20): 'the prince of the kingdom of Yavan' and 'the prince of the kingdom of Paras.' And they thought that they had the power through them to do good or harm, and each nation worshipped its own prince, for the early ones knew them. And these are what are called throughout the Torah and the Scriptures 'אלהים אחרים,' 'Elohei ha-amim' (the deities of the peoples), for the angels are called Elohim, as it is said: 'הוא אלהי האלהים' (Deuteronomy 10:17), 'השתחוו לו כל אלהים' (Psalms 97:7), 'כי גדול ה׳ מכל האלהים' (Exodus 18:11). And even though the worshippers acknowledged that the great power and the absolute might belong to El Elyon — and thus our Sages said (Menahot 110a): they called Him 'אלהא דאלהיא' (the God of gods) — it was regarding this that Scripture said 'זובח לאלהים יחרם,' referring to them by the name that is recognized.\n\nAnd the second kind of idolatry: they reverted to worshipping the visible host of heaven — some worshipping the sun or the moon, and some a constellation among the zodiac signs, for every nation knew the power of the constellation associated with it according to its governance over its land. And they thought that through their worship the constellation would grow stronger and benefit them, as it is written (Jeremiah 8:2): 'ושטחום לשמש ולירח ולכל צבא השמים אשר אהבום ואשר עבדום ואשר הלכו אחריהם ואשר דרשום ואשר השתחוו להם,' and as it is said in the Torah concerning the prohibition of idolatry (Deuteronomy 4:19): 'ופן תשא עיניך השמימה וראית את השמש ואת הירח ואת הכוכבים כל צבא השמים ונדחת והשתחוית להם ועבדתם אשר חלק ה׳ אלהיך אותם לכל העמים תחת כל השמים' — meaning: because HaShem allotted them to all the nations and gave each nation its star and constellation, you shall not be drawn after them to serve them. And these are the people who began to make the many forms in graven images and asherim and sun-pillars, for they would make images of their constellation at the hours when, according to their preeminence, they had power, and they would, according to their reasoning, confer power and success upon the people. And it seems likely to me that this began in the generation of the Dispersion, when HaShem scattered them across the lands, and the stars and constellations ruled over them according to their divisions, for the builders wanted to make a name for themselves and not be divided, as I alluded to in its place (Genesis 11:2). And all these sects had false prophets who foretold the future to them and announced some of what would befall them through the wisdom of sorcery and divination, for the constellations too have officers dwelling in the air like angels in the heaven, who know the future. And from this kind of worship, some among them worshipped human beings, for when they saw one of the children of men with great dominion and his star rising very high, like Nebuchadnezzar, the people of his land thought that by accepting his worship upon themselves and directing their intention toward him, their constellation would rise with his, and he too would think that through the attachment of their thought to him, success would be added to him through the power of their souls directed toward him. And this was the view of Pharaoh, according to the words of our Sages (Exodus Rabbah 9:7), and the view of Sennacherib, of whom Scripture records his thought: 'אעלה על במתי עב אדמה לעליון' (Isaiah 14:14), and of Hiram and his associates, who made themselves into deities, for they were wicked men, not utter ignoramuses.\n\nAnd the third kind of idolatry: afterward they reverted to worshipping the shedim (demons), which are spirits, as I shall explain with Elohim's help (Leviticus 17:7), for among them too there are those appointed over the nations, who are the masters of that land, to harm its enemies and those who stumble in it, as is known from their nature in the science of necromancy, and likewise in the words of our Sages. And regarding this Scripture says (Deuteronomy 32:17): 'יזבחו לשדים לא אלוה אלהים לא ידעום חדשים מקרוב באו לא שערום אבתיכם' — Scripture mocks them, that they sacrifice even to the shedim, which are not any deity at all, meaning that they are not like the angels who are called 'אלוה'; rather, they are 'Elohim' that they did not know, meaning that they found in them no divinity or sovereign power whatsoever. And they are 'חדשים' (new) to them, for they learned to do so from recent times, from the Egyptian sorcerers, and even their wicked ancestors such as Terach and Nimrod did not contemplate them at all. And concerning this He warns: 'ולא יזבחו עוד את זבחיהם לשעירים אשר הם זנים אחריהם' (Leviticus 17:7).\n\nBehold, the Torah prohibited in this second commandment every form of worship but to YHWH alone. And therefore He warned at the beginning: <b>לא יהיה לך אלהים אחרים על פני</b> — these being the first kind; and this is <b>על פני</b>, as I alluded to its secret. And He further warned against the graven image (פסל) and also against the likeness (תמונה), which alludes also to the imagined spiritual entity, as it is written (Job 4:16): 'יעמד ולא אכיר מראהו תמונה לנגד עיני.' And thus they said (Rosh Hashanah 24b): 'אשר בשמים' — to include the sun and moon, stars and constellations; 'ממעל' — to include the ministering angels. For even regarding them they would make forms for the separate intelligences, which are the soul of the constellations, as was the case with the matter of the golden calf, which I am destined to explain with Elohim's help (Ramban on Exodus 32:1)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :register {:flags {1 ["God"]}}, :notes {0 "The phrase 'ולא תשתחוה להם' does not appear explicitly in Exodus 20:5 in the form Ramban discusses; he is arguing about the syntactic attachment of the prohibition of bowing to the prohibition of making.", 1 "Ramban's citation of the Guide for the Perplexed is to 1:36 (not 1:32 as one might infer from the Arabic numeral in the Hebrew text, which likely refers to the chapter number in the standard numbering). The phrase 'בקדושי עליון' literally means 'in Kedoshim' (i.e., the parashah of Kedoshim), referring to instances within that Torah portion, not the tractate Sanhedrin."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:20:00.236154772Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}